 hydroxylase\"). The control set handles this construction differently. In TP 10484772, the sentence \"results in a **deficiency of norrin** (the protein product of the NDP gene)\" is left unannotated. That is the same construction, and the gold standard treats it as a molecular state, not a disorder. The single feature separating the cases is whether the gene/enzyme name is a **premodifier of a disorder-name head** (\"G6PD deficiency,\" \"Coats disease\") or a **postmodifying PP argument that names the deficient molecule** (\"deficiency of norrin\").\n- **Isolate Trigger:** The lexeme \"deficiency\" acts as a disease-cue word. Combined with a gene or enzyme name, it looks like a disorder name, so the model tags the whole phrase. The model also ignores that the PP is the argument of a causal or mechanistic clause (\"caused by,\" \"responsible for\"), where the deficiency describes how the disease works.\n- **Hypothesis Check:** The trigger explains both cases. In Example 1, the LLM span is a reversed-order re-mention of the canonical gold name \"G6PD deficiency\" (token 772–787), which is a premodifier compound. The mechanistic phrase \"enzyme deficiency in G6PD A-\" is not a disorder name. In Example 2, the snippet names the disorder (\"phenylketonuria\") and then describes its cause (\"caused by a deficiency of hepatic PAH\"). The model selected the causal phrase instead of the disorder name. (I'm inferring the gold span for Example 2 from the snippet, since its gold annotation isn't in the input.)\n\n**Step 2: Formulate Pattern**\n\n- The model labels \"deficiency + [of/in] + gene/enzyme/protein\" as SpecificDisease when the phrase is a relational or causal description of a molecular deficit. Gold reserves SpecificDisease for the nosological name, which is either a premodifier compound or a disorder named elsewhere in the text.\n\n---\n\n**Pattern Insight: Enzyme/Protein \"Deficiency of X\" as Mechanism vs. Named Disorder**\n\n- **Confusion Trigger:** The disease-cue head noun \"deficiency\" followed by a postmodifying PP (\"of/in\" + gene, enzyme, or protein name), often inside a causal frame (\"caused by,\" \"responsible for,\" \"results in\"). The model treats the phrase as a disorder name.\n- **Contrastive Evidence:** In TP 10484772, \"a deficiency of norrin (the protein product of the NDP gene)\" is correctly left unannotated because it describes a molecular state. Also in TP 10484772, the span for \"Coats' disease of the retina\" stops at \"Coats' disease,\" which shows that a trailing PP is excluded from a disease span. In 1303173, the gold SpecificDisease \"glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency\" uses the gene/enzyme as a premodifier of the head, which is the form that names a disorder.\n- **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):**\n  1. Tag \"deficiency\" as part of a SpecificDisease span only when the gene/enzyme/protein name is a **premodifier** of \"deficiency\" and the compound names a condition (e.g., \"G6PD deficiency\").\n  2. If \"deficiency\" is followed by a **postmodifying PP** (\"of/in\" + gene/enzyme/protein) in a causal, mechanistic, or lab-finding context, **do not** tag the phrase. The PP names the deficient molecule, and the disorder is tagged separately wherever it is named (e.g., \"phenylketonuria\").\n  3. Never extend a SpecificDisease span across a trailing PP (\"Coats' disease **of the retina**\" → span ends at \"disease\").\n  4. Adjectival \"deficient\" or \"deficiency-related\" describing a phenotype is a Modifier, not a SpecificDisease (cf. \"G6PD deficient\" in 1303173).", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4165, "output_tokens": 9570, "output_t